Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:02.230 align:start position:0%
 
you're watching the daily climate show  on sky news  on today's programme a record number of  people are forced to leave their homes  because of disasters linked  to climate change our warmer  temperatures could be opening up  new divisions in the arctic  and we meet the award-winning ghanaian  environmental activist  campaigning against coal  hello and welcome to the uk's only daily  climate news show where we track the  changes happening to our world right now  and meet those developing the solutions  and coming up why angelina jolie is  calling for more women to become  beekeepers but first climate disasters  caused  more internal displacement than war last  year  the norwegian refugee council's internal  displacement monitoring centre  found three times the number of people  were forced to leave their homes by  events such as storms  floods and wildfires than by violent  conflict  last year a total of 40.5 million people  were internally displaced that's the  highest level  on record of that more than 30 million  were displaced by disasters such as  cyclones storms floods or wildfires  while 9.8 million people were forced  from their homes because of conflict or  violence  and if we look at which countries had  the highest number of disaster driven  internal displacements  afghanistan is at the top with more than  a million displaced due to  weather-related or geophysical events  then india is next followed by pakistan  ethiopia  and sudan well africa correspondent john  sparks is here with  more so john just how much of an issue  is this for africa  hi and yeah this is an absolutely huge  issue as this report  makes clear it's a sort of annual census  for the uh displaced  earlier this year we saw 700 000 people  who'd fled from their homes in northern  mozambique they were forced to  more southerly area trying to eke out a  livelihood  in an agricultural area that really  wasn't fit for fit for farming  and these weather-related events they  form a big part of this  report in central and eastern africa  people have been  suffering from two years of  unprecedented rainfall and  flooding the continent's largest lake  lake victoria now experiencing  record breaking water levels that has  flooded out communities in uganda  and kenya and it has sent a huge amount  of water  down the nile that water has broken the  banks  in countries like south sudan and we've  just returned from a reporting trip  there  we saw huge agricultural areas turned  into a  inland sea with farmers who had used to  work the agricultural land there turned  into fishermen trying to eke out  survival fishing  on their fields quite extraordinary  scenes john sparks thank you  in today's other climate news the  european commission has approved a 22.5  billion euro polish scheme to develop  green energy  under new plans an offshore wind farm  will be built along the country's  coastline to expand its renewable power  capacity  at the moment more than 70 percent of  poland's power is generated from coal  [Applause]  the national trust will create 5 000  acres of new woodland over the next four  years the charity will use a four  million pound donation from hsbc to  plant  two million trees which they say will  lock in over a million tons of carbon  from the atmosphere  it's part of efforts to create woodland  areas that will regenerate naturally in  the future  a giant piece of ice has broken off  antarctica to become the world's  largest iceberg the european space  agency confirmed the slab of ice  similar in size to the island of majorca  has dropped off the western edge of the  ron  ice shelf into the weddle sea the  iceberg is officially known as the a76  after the  antarctic quadrant where it was first  seen  and you can hear more about this story  with climate scientist dr ella gilbert  on our instagram  at sky news  [Applause]  and it's world b day and angelina jolie  is encouraging people to protect  our fuzzy friends the actor is part of  women in bees a  five-year program launched by the un  that trains women to be beekeepers  angelina says teaching women these  skills will benefit their  local communities as bees are vital to  our environment  now the arctic is believed to hold up to  a quarter of the earth's undiscovered  oil and gas and with the impact of  climate change  melting ice is offering new  opportunities to exploit natural  resources  and open up shipping routes over the  past two days foreign ministers from  arctic nations have come together at the  arctic  council which will be chaired by russia  for the next two years  it is important to focus on preserving  the biodiversity of the arctic its  unique ecosystem  on preventing marine and land pollution  we are prepared for  improving our practical interaction to  jointly respond to these challenges and  a number of others  against the backdrop of climate change  ensuring sustainable and safe  maritime activities in the arctic is of  particular importance  with the rapid development of navigation  we see  diversification of transport routes  between the atlantic and the asia  pacific region  well moscow correspondent diana magna  joins us now so diana what difference  could  russian chairmanship of the arctic  council make  well i think russia is the largest  arctic state so it stands to  suffer the most from climate change  especially in terms of melting  permafrost  but also to benefit in large part from  what you've just mentioned  possible mineral extraction as the ice  melts in the north and also  the opening of shipping lanes the  northern sea route  and the arctic council has always been  quite an effective forum of  cooperation between the arctic states  but there have been concerns that  russia's  military buildup in the arctic and that  concern goes both ways too  russia is extremely concerned about nato  exercises  close to its northern border says that  it should be able to defend its arctic  coast  especially as the ice retreats  and today said that it wanted to revive  military talks  as part of the arctic council's agenda  now whether that actually happens is  another story  they were suspended in 2014 and the  other arctic states  may not be particularly keen but  fundamentally today we heard  that there is a commitment to wanting to  keep this a place of low tension  and all the states are behind that diana  magnay thank you  now sky ocean rescue and the wwf are  launching an  interactive map to raise awareness of  what lives on our coastlines  and they found more than 80 percent of  people are unaware of just how many  marine species are living in the uk  seas it includes more than 40 species of  shark  atlantic puffins and bottlenose dolphins  wwf's executive director of advocacy and  campaigns kate norgrove  explains how the map will work  it's going to help marine enthusiasts  all over the country for orcas seagrass  water marshes seals whales dolphins  to name but a few and and can illustrate  how their habitats are under threat  and the map also lifts out ways that the  public can do uh can help to do their  bit to preserve the uk's ocean  and you can take a look at the sky ocean  rescue interactive map for yourself just  head to wwf.org dot uk  ocean heroes now  strawberry season has officially begun  in the uk but it's later than usual  due to the cooler winter and spring that  we've had it should have started at the  beginning of may but it's only this week  that some of the berries  are now ready for picking there's an  advantage to this because the longer  they spend ripening  the bigger and juicier the fruits are  about  70 percent of all strawberries bought in  the uk are grown by  farmers here but the unpredictability of  growing conditions due to climate change  is having an effect now if strawberries  from the uk  aren't available then they're imported  usually from spain the netherlands and  belgium there's a way that strawberries  can be harvested here almost all year  round by growing them in big plastic  tunnels or glass houses  but that of course needs artificial heat  and electric heaters are only as green  as their fuel supply well animal making  jones is a farmer in harrogate and she  joins me now um  welcome to you i know that you aim to be  carbon neutral in your strawberry  growing so what does that involve  so we are under tunnel but outside so we  don't use any artificial heat  we literally rely on the sunlight to  warm  the tunnels which is generally about two  degrees warmer  than the outside weather and the impact  it's had on us is that we've had frost  uh up until early may and it has damaged  some of the flowers which has killed  some of the  some of the fruit already so although  we're going to see bigger fruit with  what's left  we've already lost quite a large amount  of crop  and am i right i'm thinking that you've  got different species to try to  lengthen out the season as long as you  can  yes you grow several varieties  throughout the season  to try and get that may to october um  for supply and to retailers however  if it becomes cold earlier in september  your season is  shortened hugely by you know three weeks  late in may and then  not getting to mid-october you lose all  of that yield which means you know  you're not producing as much fruit and  therefore your cost goes up  and tell us a bit more about your  sustainability because i know you go to  some lengths  to make sure that you are sustainable  yeah so we're doing many things so using  our waste rhubarb waste strawberries and  turning them into jams and chutneys  we also make non-alcoholic low sugar  healthy drinks  and freeze them for the catering  industry so we are trying to collaborate  with other people too so a gin company i  use my strawberries with  to try and use every little bit that  we've got and really reduce our carbon  footprint  and then within the business we have  solar panels and we catch all our own  rain water so  we're totally self-sufficient annabelle  making jones thanks very much indeed  now in the latest of our series of  climate diaries we're meeting chabazi  ezekiel  it was because of his four year long  campaign that the ghanaian environment  ministry  cancelled the construction of a huge  coal power plant  and now he's working with youngsters to  help them get involved in the fight  against climate change  we are at a breaking point and we need  to work  now and fast even though young people  are vulnerable to the input of climate  change  young people can also become the change  agents that we need to address  the climate impact when the government  expressed his desire to build a copland  i led the campaign you know again and  koplan proposing  renewable energy as a better alternative  as compared  to coal plant  one of the things i've realized as a  campaigner is that  when you oppose comments or when you  critique comments decision  it is also important to provide an  alternative you know to make you a  relevant contributor in the process  so whilst we're campaigning against  school we are proposing renewable  energies as better alternatives  i have seen farmers losing their farm  because of severe landfall patterns  the whole community actually submerged  by the sea  because of sea level rise now seeing  people migrating because of the severe  impact of climate change  in my campaign or in my advocacy work i  work largely with young people  so that their voices can be heard so  they can also influence  decision making from the local level to  the national level and thankfully  we are making progress in our regard the  kumamoto gonna  listen to us and now stop the planet  with equipment and now focusing more  on developing and utilizing renewable  energies in ghana  and this process is purely generated  created implemented and deployed by  young people to showcase that young  people  have what it takes to prove their  climate agenda  that's all from us tomorrow on the daily  climate show why brazil's environment  minister is being investigated  over claims of illegal timber exports  thanks for watching  see you then  you

